Article 8 With the aim of preventing and controlling risks, the holder shall include key pharmacovigilance activities in the quality assurance system, with particular consideration of the following:
(1) setting up a reasonable organisational structure;
(2) providing the personnel, equipment and resources needed for pharmacovigilance activities;
(3) formulating management policies that meet legal and regulatory requirements;
(4) formulating comprehensive, clear and workable standard operating procedures;
(5) establishing effective and unobstructed channels for collecting information on suspected adverse drug reactions;
(6) conducting reporting and handling activities that meet legal and regulatory requirements;
(7) conducting effective activities to identify and assess risk signals;
(8) taking effective control measures for identified risks;
(9) ensuring that pharmacovigilance-related documents and records are accessible, retrievable and traceable.

Article 9 The holder shall set, and update in good time, pharmacovigilance quality control indicators; the control indicators shall run through the key pharmacovigilance activities and be broken down and assigned to specific departments and persons, including but not limited to:
(1) compliance of adverse drug reaction reporting;
(2) compliance of periodic safety update reports;
(3) timeliness of signal detection and evaluation;
(4) timeliness of updates of the pharmacovigilance system master file;
(5) formulation and implementation of pharmacovigilance plans;
(6) formulation and implementation of personnel training plans.

Article 21 The pharmacovigilance department shall perform the following main duties:
(1) collection, handling and reporting of information on suspected adverse drug reactions;
(2) identifying and assessing drug risks, making risk management recommendations, and organising or participating in risk control, risk communication and other activities;
(3) organising the preparation of the pharmacovigilance system master file, periodic safety update reports, pharmacovigilance plans, etc.;
(4) organising or participating in post-marketing drug safety studies;
(5) organising or assisting with pharmacovigilance-related communication, education and training;
(6) other pharmacovigilance-related work.

Article 25 The person responsible for pharmacovigilance is responsible for the operation and continuous improvement of the pharmacovigilance system, ensures that the pharmacovigilance system meets the requirements of relevant laws and regulations and of this Practice, and has the following main duties:
(1) ensuring the compliance of adverse drug reaction monitoring and reporting;
(2) overseeing the identification, assessment and control of drug safety risks and ensuring the effective implementation of risk-control measures;
(3) being responsible for managing the communication of drug safety information and ensuring that communication is timely and effective;
(4) ensuring unobstructed communication channels within the holder and with drug regulatory departments and adverse drug reaction monitoring institutions;
(5) being responsible for reviewing or signing off important pharmacovigilance documents.

Article 30 Where the holder uses computerised systems to conduct pharmacovigilance activities, the following requirements shall be met:
(1) management requirements for the design, installation, configuration, validation, testing, training, use and maintenance of the computerised system shall be defined, and these processes shall be recorded in a standardised manner;
(2) security management requirements for the computerised system shall be defined, and controls such as access control, assignment of privileges, audit trail, authorised changes and electronic signatures shall be chosen according to the level, to ensure the security of the computerised system and its data;
(3) the computerised system shall have complete data security and confidentiality functions to ensure that electronic data are not damaged, lost or disclosed, and shall be appropriately validated or qualified to demonstrate that it is fit for its intended use.

Article 44 The holder shall evaluate the seriousness of adverse drug reactions. A reaction meeting any of the following shall be evaluated as a serious adverse drug reaction:
(1) results in death;
(2) is life-threatening (meaning that the patient was at risk of death at the time the adverse drug reaction occurred, not that death might have occurred had the reaction worsened further);
(3) results in hospitalisation or prolongation of hospitalisation;
(4) results in permanent or significant disability or loss of function;
(5) results in a congenital anomaly or birth defect;
(6) results in another important medical event which, without treatment, might lead to one of the situations listed above.

Article 58 When conducting signal detection, the holder shall focus on the following signals:
(1) adverse drug reactions not mentioned in the package insert, especially serious adverse drug reactions;
(2) adverse drug reactions mentioned in the package insert whose frequency, severity, etc. has increased markedly;
(3) adverse drug reactions caused by suspected new drug-drug, drug-device or drug-food interactions;
(4) suspected new use in special populations or changes in known use in special populations;
(5) suspected adverse reactions showing clustering where a relationship with drug quality cannot be excluded.

Article 59 The holder shall prioritise signals. Signals that may affect the benefit-risk balance of the product or have an impact on public health shall be evaluated with priority. Signal prioritisation may consider the following factors:
(1) the seriousness, severity, outcome, reversibility and preventability of the adverse drug reaction;
(2) patient exposure and the expected frequency of the adverse drug reaction;
(3) patient exposure in high-risk populations and in populations with different patterns of use;
(4) the impact of treatment interruption on patients and the availability of other treatment options;
(5) the risk-control measures expected to be taken;
(6) signals applicable to other drugs of the same class.

Article 95 Communication shall be carried out urgently in the following situations:
(1) the drug carries a safety risk of which healthcare professionals and patients need to be informed urgently, but the package insert of products in circulation cannot be updated in time;
(2) there is irrational drug use that cannot be corrected by revising the package insert and that may lead to serious consequences;
(3) other situations that may have a major impact on patient or public health.

Article 106 The pharmacovigilance system master file shall include at least the following:
(1) organisation: describing the organisational structure, responsibilities and interrelationships related to PV activities;
(2) basic information on the PV responsible person: including region of residence, contact details, CV, responsibilities, etc.;
(3) full-time staffing: including number of full-time staff, professional backgrounds, responsibilities, etc.;
(4) sources of suspected ADR information: describing the main channels and methods of collecting suspected ADR information;
(5) IT tools or systems: describing the IT tools or systems used for PV activities;
(6) management policies and SOPs: a brief description of the PV management policies and a list of PV management policies and SOPs;
(7) operation of the PV system: describing ADR monitoring and reporting and the identification, assessment and control of drug risks;
(8) contracted PV activities: listing the content, duration and contractors, with a list of contract agreements;
(9) quality management: describing PV quality management, including quality objectives, quality assurance system, quality control indicators, internal audits, etc.;
(10) annexes: including policy and procedure documents, product list, contract agreements, internal audit reports, master file revision log, etc.
